{"kind":"request","text":"Attack-surface review of dap at main 7177b87b: reachability findings and the remediation to file\n\nRead-only review of main 7177b87be0 on 2026-11-10, by the reachability method of Anthropic's \"attack surface reduction\" article (https://www.anthropic.com/research/attack-surface-reduction): an entry-point inventory, then three passes (default configuration, early reachability, configuration interaction), each finding confirmed by a trace where a trace is possible without a deployed Worker. This review succeeds the review of 21 October (request 6dd06d03, main 603220b9) and restates the status of each of its findings.\n\nEvidence labels. TRACED: the real source module ran in Node and showed the behavior. VERIFIED: the planner session re-read every cited line. REPORTED: one of five code inventories cited the lines and the planner session did not re-read them. OBSERVED: read from a live Worker on 2026-11-10 between 23:05 and 23:20 EST. INFERRED: not checked. Evidence directory: ~/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-10-attack-surface-review (scripts, outputs, SHA256SUMS). Citations are paths at 7177b87b.\n\nHeadline. The October remediation closed the large pre-authentication paths: enforce mode keeps an unknown scope ID away from every object, the grant check runs before foreign reads, the page key cannot be exported, application screens left the room origin, and hosted MCP is closed unless a setting opens it. No injection path and no way to read another room's content was found. The open risk has moved to availability. The serving limits that R12 added refuse every new caller when their tables fill, and one sender can fill them.\n\n== 0. What a caller reaches, measured ==\n\nMeasured by a static import closure (regex over import statements, lines of .ts source with comments; graph.mjs). The room Worker entry apps/room/fetch.ts links 583 modules and 91,832 lines. The figures bound what a class of caller can reach. They are module-level upper bounds, not executed-line counts.\n\n- Anonymous, no key. /health; the public Site of a published room (240 reads per address per minute); the room page and brand files; a GET of any scope route in enforce mode, which costs one index lookup. The Site path runs the Markdown renderer (6 modules, 2,098 lines) and the Git HTTP reader with its pack decoder (38 modules, 4,043 lines) on content that members wrote and on bytes that the Git host returned. These 6,100 lines are the parsers an anonymous caller drives.\n- Self-signed: any fresh keypair. POST /v1/scopes (1 MiB body, canonical parse, signature check, serving window); signed reads; acts on any known scope up to the grant check; `found` on an open register; the census refusal path.\n- Behind a switch that every checked-in configuration leaves off. Hosted MCP and OAuth: 51 modules, 9,151 lines, 10.0% of the closure, behind `MCP_ENABLED` (404 before any body is read). OBSERVED: /mcp and both OAuth discovery paths answer 404 on dap-demo, dap-grow, dap-lending, artroom-scope and dap-connect. GitHub host: 931 lines, wired by no checked-in configuration.\n- Linked into every room Worker, wired on one. Archive: 93 modules, 11,097 lines, 12.1% of the closure. Only dap-grow sets GROW_ARCHIVE_CONFIG. INFERRED that no other Worker reaches it.\n\n== 1. Pass 2, early reachability: work before the gate ==\n\n1.1 HIGH (availability). One address fills the serving-window table and every new caller is refused. TRACED (trace.mjs, traces 1 and 2). ServingLimits.account makes a key window for every fresh verified key, including when the sender's address window is already past its limit (packages/scope/src/serving-limits.ts:102-114). Creation windows and signed-read windows share one cap of 8,192, and a full table refuses every identity that has no window (:107). Creation windows last one hour (:97). The serving check runs before parentlessCreationAdmission, so a creation that the Worker then refuses as unauthorized still takes a window (packages/scope/src/worker.ts:291-305). Trace: 8,191 requests from one address, 30 admitted, table full; a new key at a new address is refused creation and signed reads until 61 minutes later. A reader with live windows keeps them, loses them when they end (one minute) if the sender takes the freed slots, and is then refused too. Reduction: allocate no key window when the address window is past its limit; separate caps for creation and reads; at capacity, count new identities in one shared overflow window instead of refusing them.\n\n1.2 MEDIUM. The public Site window keys the raw address, so the addresses of one IPv6 /64 fill it. TRACED (trace 3). siteReadAddressKey returns the address text (packages/scope/src/site/window.ts:9-14); every other window uses addressKey, which maps a /64 to one key (packages/bytes/src/address.ts:43-46). The table holds 4,096 keys and refuses a new key when full (window.ts:33-36). Trace: 4,096 addresses of one /64 all admitted, one visitor elsewhere refused until the minute ends; addressKey gives those addresses one key. Reduction: key the Site window with addressKey, and apply the overflow rule of 1.1.\n\n1.3 MEDIUM, in use on dap-grow. The sandbox service path drops the caller's address. VERIFIED. The sandbox Worker forwards cf-connecting-ip and its comment says that preserves per-address limits (apps/sandbox/worker.ts:62-65). ApplicationScreenService.native ignores the header: it calls site(request, env) with no address and route(..., null, ...) (packages/scope/src/worker.ts:762-766). A null address is the one shared Site bucket \"missing\" (site/window.ts:10), so one visitor's 240 reads close sandbox Site reads for everyone. The gateway admits acts, sessions and reads for any scope ID on that Worker, not only the installation's room (packages/client/src/application-gateway.ts:53-65), so through a screen host fresh keys face no address window on signed reads or joins. OBSERVED: dap-grow's live APPLICATION_SCREEN_HOSTS holds the grow.inguz.space row; the other four hold []. Reduction: pass the edge address as an explicit argument of the service call, or restrict gateway scope IDs to the installation's room; correct the comment.\n\n1.4 MEDIUM. The one ScopeIndex object does the expensive work before the cheap decision. Creation order VERIFIED, the rest REPORTED, throughput INFERRED. For each creation it parses and canonicalizes up to 1 MiB and checks the signature before it reads the address window, also for an address already past its limit (packages/scope/src/serving-admission.ts:21,41-43). Every scope call in enforce mode asks it `has` (scope-index.ts:144); the Site windows and Site tokens live in it too (worker.ts:165,172). An anonymous GET /v1/scopes/\u003cid\u003e has no window. Reduction: check size and signature in the Worker and send the object only a key and an address; refuse an over-limit address before the parse; answer `has` from a cache.\n\n1.5 MEDIUM, live. artroom-scope runs the scope index in register mode. OBSERVED: live SCOPE_INDEX_MODE is \"register\" (version fefa9258); the other four Workers run \"enforce\". The checked-in file sets it (apps/room/wrangler.artroom-scope.jsonc:13). In register mode an unknown well-formed scope ID reaches registerKnown() on a new object (packages/scope/src/scope-index.ts:145-151), which writes the full schema, with no window. This is October finding 1.2, still open on this Worker. Reduction: finish the listed sweep, commit \"enforce\" in the file, and deploy.\n\n1.6 LOW to MEDIUM. `found` on an open register has no serving window. Path REPORTED; provider calls INFERRED. Serving windows cover parentless creation and signed reads only (serving-admission.ts:11); submit has a window for joins only (object.ts:603-609). On a register whose founding policy is `open` (dap-demo's Start register, by design) any fresh key founds rooms without bound: four objects and a claim row each, and a repository where the host is Artifacts. Reduction: a per-key and per-address window for register `found`, after 1.1.\n\n1.7 LOW. Checks that run after the work they guard. REPORTED except as marked.\n- Census: the register proof read (up to 1 MiB, up to 10 s) runs before the actor check (scope-index-census.ts:63 before :73).\n- Hosted MCP `grant`: the Worker resolves the OAuth client, which can fetch an outside document, before any signature check (worker-mcp.ts:110-115; VERIFIED). In register mode the control route also calls registerKnown on a membership ID from an unverified header (worker-mcp.ts:89-100).\n- Acts with native grant marks (`found`, `join`) and acts whose standing read came back null fetch up to usesPerEntry foreign facts before the judge refuses (core.ts:589-633). The facts come from indexed scopes only.\n- A refused act returns judgedAt {seq, hash}, so any caller who knows a scope ID reads the room's head (core.ts:571-632).\n\n1.8 LOW. The anonymous Site path does more parsing than a page needs. REPORTED; cost INFERRED. A Site view asks for the commit with no shallow bound, so the host returns the ancestry closure up to 16 MiB and the pure-JS scanner walks it (git/src/http-read.ts:895-921, 197-262); closureObjects 4096 fails Site for a room whose history passes 4,096 objects. The isolated shallow mode exists (http-read.ts:862-894). For a GitHub host each view also spends the deployment's static read token on an identity call with no cache (site/host.ts:215-229). The Site token table holds 256 keys for the whole deployment and answers 503 when full (site/token-reuse.ts:101-102).\n\n== 2. Pass 3, configuration interaction ==\n\n2.1 MEDIUM-LOW. A self-host Worker serves open creation between its deploy and its secrets. TRACED (trace4.mjs) and VERIFIED. The guide deploys first and sets DEPLOYMENT and SESSION_SECRET in a second command (docs/self-host-cloudflare.md:74-80; docs/deploy.md:48). With DEPLOYMENT unset the code treats the Worker as local and admits any parentless creation (packages/scope/src/creation-admission.ts:32-36); child creation skips the lineage walk (creation-lineage.ts:113-116). Trace: settings {} admit a directory `found`; DEPLOYMENT set and no register pin refuses. Rooms made in the window stay known scopes, and their memberships issue sessions under the operator's secret afterwards. /health answers 503 in the window and names no cause. Reduction: put DEPLOYMENT in the template's vars (it is no secret), or fail closed unless an explicit local flag is set; fix the documented order.\n\n2.2 MEDIUM, a governance fact. One admin key republishes a room's rules with no second key. VERIFIED. `publish` needs the grant rules.publish and shape guards only (packages/platform/src/rules-scope.ts:206-229); the admin role alone holds it (membership.ts:110). One publish sets approvals 0, ownerMayReview true and extents at 0 approvals with a catch-all; landings outside the `rules` extent then need no review. The fixed minimum of the `rules` extent governs files, not this act. rules@10 is the default cohort; the certified policy that closes the permissive extent forms (rules-policy-v11.ts:89-94, rules@11 and @12) is in no default cohort. The threat model does not state that any one admin governs the rules. Decision for the planner: state it, or require a counted review from a second holder of rules.publish in the next rules version.\n\n2.3 LOW. /health answers 200 while a weakening setting is in force. REPORTED. health.ts:62-80 reads none of SCOPE_INDEX_MODE, MCP_ENABLED, ALLOW_OPEN_CREATION, PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS, the serving limits or APPLICATION_TRUST. Finding 1.5 is the live case: nothing but a version read shows that artroom-scope runs register mode. --keep-vars keeps a test-time ALLOW_OPEN_CREATION across every later deploy. Reduction: a `policy` member in /health that names index mode, MCP enablement and open creation.\n\n2.4 LOW. Remaining pairs. REPORTED except as marked.\n- REGISTER_SCOPE is compared with ARTIFACTS_CONFIG only; a GitHub register that differs goes undetected unless INSTALLED_REGISTER_SCOPES is set (register-setting.ts:13-22; github-wiring.ts:101).\n- Founding policy `open` still passes without reading a `founders` list (platform/src/register.ts:294-300). October 3.12, open.\n- When extents exist the destination judges only the checks that extents name, not checks marked `required` (reservation.ts:271-289). October 3.4, open.\n- The fixed one-hour read token applies to destination@19 only (version-questions.ts:236-238; VERIFIED). dap-connect pins destination@20 and dap-commons pins destination@16, which accept 1 to 24 hours (wrangler.connect.jsonc:21, wrangler.commons.jsonc:14). A key about to be revoked keeps repository read for 24 hours there. trust-boundaries.md:74 calls those pins historical.\n- The Mac self-host kit requires MCP_ENABLED \"true\" (apps/room/celld/mac/deploy.py:148-149; VERIFIED). An operator cannot run it with hosted MCP closed. celld also accepts CF-Connecting-IP from any local process that calls 127.0.0.1:28900 directly (kit.py:344).\n- dap-lending's creation pins list membership@4 and rules@3 (wrangler.lending.jsonc:20); INFERRED to have no effect. The sandbox configs leave preview_urls unset. dap-home serves workers.dev beside its custom domain (static only).\n\n== 3. Pass 1, default configuration, and the surfaces behind MCP_ENABLED ==\n\nThe default is sound: the checked-in configurations open nothing that the default deployment does not use, apart from 1.5. The findings below apply when an operator opens hosted MCP. File them before the O4 live window.\n\n3.1 MEDIUM when open. Any active member uses up a room's hosted MCP control budget for good. VERIFIED. Each control operation reserves 4 KiB (packages/mcp/src/hosted.ts:231). The read-only `connections` control needs an active key only and stores a row (hosted.ts:354-364). expire() credits the answer body and keeps the 4 KiB tombstone; no code deletes a control row (hosted.ts:259-268). The budget is 16 MiB less 1 MiB reserved for retire (hosted.ts:50-53). The consent page signs one `connections` call per load and one per agent (apps/room/page/src/oauth-context.ts:90,106), so ordinary use spends the budget too. When it is spent the registry answers 507 to create, grant and connections, and no path replaces a membership's registry. Reduction: store no row for `connections`, or release the tombstone after its notAfter window; add a per-key window on control routes.\n\n3.2 LOW. An anonymous caller makes the Worker fetch client metadata without bound. VERIFIED. GET /oauth/authorize/context resolves any client_id on an allowed origin (packages/scope/src/oauth-routes.ts:41-46); an invalid document is not cached, so every call fetches again, and a full pending table of 1,000 answers 503 to every user (packages/mcp/src/oauth-clients.ts:108-127). Reduction: a short negative cache and an address window.\n\n3.3 LOW. The role-subset rule holds at grant issue only. VERIFIED for the check site (hosted.ts:551). Bearer rotation, create, refresh and access standing run authorityOf or grant standing with no subset check (hosted.ts:143-154, 532-541; oauth-access.ts:135-147). A controller whose agent role an admin later widens keeps the wider actions. docs/hosted-mcp.md:184,293 describes the rule as general.\n\n3.4 LOW, INFERRED on host behavior. A trusted package's card script shares the document of the tool bridge and can post tools/call itself; the write tools carry no _meta.ui.visibility (packages/mcp/src/service.ts:211; views/tools.ts:53). Reduction: mark write tools model-only.\n\n3.5 INFO. grantExisting leaves the client mapping after a refused grant (hosted.ts:340-345). `credential` returns its secret in structuredContent, which some hosts show the model (service.ts:1703-1705). Unknown guard forms still render as raw JSON in tool descriptions, capped at 1,000 characters (client/src/describe.ts:71,77,337).\n\n== 4. Client, browser and credentials (all LOW or INFO, REPORTED except as marked) ==\n\n- `dap issues` writes issue titles raw to the terminal; a member can plant escape sequences (cli/src/command-issues.ts:137; VERIFIED; text fields accept control characters, bytes/src/field-value.ts:30). git/src/diff.ts shownPath already has the escaping rule.\n- `dap clone` saves and uses any HTTPS remote that the credential answer names (cli/src/command-repository.ts:188-192; VERIFIED). Only --archive checks the remote.\n- No Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy on /page/ and /oauth/authorize (apps/room/page/route.ts:29-31). Site pages on the room origin allow https: images and carry no `sandbox` directive (site/route.ts:130).\n- Member mintRead on Artifacts accepts a reply with no scope and skips the remote identity check that the other mint paths make (artifacts-host.ts:314-324). Workspace and staging token plaintext stays after expiry when a revoke is unconfirmed (workspace-host.ts:98-168). The CLI keeps spent invitation secrets (cli/src/files.ts:95-102). The Counter screen leaves an invitation in the URL fragment after a failed enrol (apps/counter/browser/main.ts:80-89).\n\n== 5. Status of the October findings ==\n\nClosed: 2.1, 2.5, 3.1, 3.3 (new scopes), 3.6, 3.7, 3.8, 3.10, 3.11.\nPartly closed: 1.1 (see 1.6 and 2.1), 1.3 (see 1.7), 1.4 (see 1.2 and 1.8), 1.5 (see 1.7 and 3.1), 1.6 (see 1.1 and 1.3), 2.2 (operator pins can list old versions), 2.3 (demo and page routes still dispatch on every Worker; logs at 100%), 2.4 (see 2.4 here), 3.2 (open in the default rules@10: approvals 0 and any-action approver, extents.ts:121-122), 3.5 (see 2.4), 3.9 (see 1.3).\nOpen: 1.2 on artroom-scope (see 1.5), 3.4, 3.12, 3.13 (shared Artifacts namespace, bucket and dataset between dap-grow and artroom-scope; now documented).\n\n== 6. What is right (keep) ==\n\nIn enforce mode an unknown ID reaches no target. The grant check runs before receiver and foreign reads; only the alarm drains timed work; the authority cache holds 4,096 keys. Session MAC compared in constant time before the parse. No API sets CORS headers or cookies, so a hostile page has no ambient authority. The page key is non-extractable and lives in IndexedDB. The room page and the cards build DOM with text nodes only; the Site renderer escapes raw HTML and its policy runs no script; raw files answer with `sandbox`. OAuth: S256 required, codes single use for 60 s, refresh rotation with reuse detection, exact redirect match, audience bound to the resource, dynamic registration off. Static bearer and tokens compared as digests. Trust is checked before any application fetch, and a release that leaves APPLICATION_TRUST refuses fresh acts. Pack decoding bounds sizes, deltas and counts and refuses thin packs; HTTP reads refuse redirects. CLI custody is 0600 under 0700; the token never enters argv. CI runs on pull_request with read permissions and actions pinned by SHA; dependencies are pinned with integrity; no secret is committed.\n\n== 7. Code and page disagreements (one documents head) ==\n\n- docs/security/entry-points.md:156-157 says churn cannot evict a live counter and omits that one address fills the table (1.1). Lines 213 and 279 state 240 per address per minute; for IPv6 the bound is per /128 (1.2). Line 283: a refused closed admission still allocates windows.\n- docs/security/threat-model.md:66 says register mode reaches legacy targets; it activates any well-formed ID. Neither page says that artroom-scope's file selects register mode (1.5). trust-boundaries.md:220 also omits it.\n- apps/sandbox/worker.ts:62-65 comment contradicts the code (1.3).\n- entry-points.md:230,296 class POST acts as `membership`; `found` on an open register is self-signed (1.6).\n- docs/self-host-cloudflare.md:74-80 and docs/deploy.md:48 order (2.1). docs/security/definition-authors.md:94 omits that one admin's publish resets the other thresholds (2.2).\n- docs/hosted-mcp.md:184,293 and notes/2026-11-02-hosted-mcp-oauth.md:89-95 subset rule (3.3). notes/2026-10-13-hosted-mcp-endpoint.md:510-526 says only a defect fills the budget and names a recovery that the registry lacks (3.1).\n- docs/hosts.md:228-233 and trust-boundaries.md:74 read-token hours (2.4). docs/hosts.md:120-123 mint reply scope. trust-boundaries.md:252 celld settings and forced MCP. docs/application-views.md:452-456 surface and tool bridge (3.4).\n\n== 8. Not covered ==\n\nNo deployed Worker was traced under load, and no request was sent to a live Worker beyond /health, six GET or POST status probes per Worker and a read of live nonsecret settings. Not read line by line: packages/define, contract, checkers, package-graph and replay; derive marks.ts, state.ts and capability/hold.ts; the membership invitation flows; rules@13 and @14; most archive*.ts; operations.ts; mcp views, wire.ts, reads.ts and custody; apps/jam; the Counter projection; scripts/control.mjs, gate.sh and public-release. The order of checks inside derive genesis.ts and judge.ts was not traced. Retained files have no code yet.\n\n== 9. Remediation to file (builder, with checker review) ==\n\nS1 Serving windows, first: 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4 and 1.6 in one head. Witness: trace.mjs traces 1 to 3 as tests that fail at 7177b87b.\nS2 artroom-scope to enforce (planner: sweep, file change, deploy), with /health `policy` (2.3) and the GitHub register comparison (2.4).\nS3 Self-host: DEPLOYMENT fail-closed and the guide's order (2.1); the Mac kit accepts MCP closed; celld address trust (2.4).\nS4 Hosted MCP, before the O4 live window: 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5 and the two MCP items of 1.7.\nS5 Check order: census actor before proof; foreign reads for native-mark acts; judgedAt on refusals (1.7).\nS6 Tokens and CLI: fixed hour for the connect and commons cohorts (2.4), and section 4.\nS7 Site: shallow read mode, GitHub identity cache, Site token table (1.8).\nS8 Rules and register forms for the next creation family: 2.2, October 3.2, 3.4 and 3.12. The planner rules on 2.2 first.\nS9 One documents head for section 7.\nOrder: S1 and S2 now (both concern live Workers); S3 and S4 before any public self-host or MCP window; S8 with the cutover family; the rest by capacity.\n\n== 10. Instruction to planner ==\n\nFile the remediation requests to the builder, grouped as above or as the planner judges, each with acceptance conditions that name the finding it closes and a witness test, and with checker review on every head. Rule on 2.2. Do S2 as planner work. Answer with a report on this request that lists the filed request IDs, their grouping and their order.","body":{"conditions":"The planner files the remediation requests of section 9 to the builder with checker review, rules on finding 2.2, does S2 as planner work, and answers with a report on this request that lists the filed request IDs, their grouping and their order.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}